qualification.scope_statement:
X4 qualifies as a source-bound nonauthor (xAI) check of A4 under four conditions: (1) it is bound to P sha256 9350ad6e... / 40,261 B, lines 115-136, where l.137 is empty; (2) it holds on the small-r set; (3) it imports A2's I3a-I3d (P l.105, 93-94/103, 109, 107-109) and A1's l.61 without re-proving them; (4) it takes the M3/M4 inequality conditional on the B_r bound.

FULL DEPTH holds for:
- (4.1), with the sup over r restricted to the small-r set;
- the (4.2) regression construction mu_r, B_r, g_r and the normal-equation orthogonality of every D^a g_r(z) to every entry of A_r (l.121-123);
- independence of finite collections and the countable-dense / separable-C^4 upgrade to independence of the whole residual field from A_r (l.125 first clause, l.127);
- uniformly bounded C^4 moments of the residual (l.125 last sentence);
- J_r >= 1, J_r independent of A_r, and sup||J_r||_p < infinity (l.129, 133-134);
- the decomposition argument giving M3, M4 <= K_0(J_r + ||A_r||_F) from the B_r bound (l.130-132);
- no inverse Hessian moment (l.136).

NOT FULL DEPTH:
- (a) P l.125's claim that B_r and its derivatives through order 4 are uniformly bounded. X4 does not argue, and does not import, the uniform-in-r boundedness of Cov(D^b f(z), U_r) and Cov(U_r, A_r) with T_r's r^-k-scaled rows (P l.103). It says only "uniformly in z by the Section 2 series".
- (b) P l.129's comparison of the cap theorem's partial-block norms with the coordinate norm. X4 takes this from P's own sentence without binding or reading CAP.

Both gaps close without new hypotheses: by Cauchy-Schwarz under Q, or by the per-mode estimate |U_k(e_omega)| <= omega^k that I checked; and by CAP l.15-19. Closing them would take a supplement written by someone else, so it would not count as X4's own derivation. No error affecting A4 was found.

